WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
how do you spot a hero  a hero stands up for others they prevent  destruction caused by powerful  technologies forces of nature or human  folly they protect life  we might now picture a fictional  character but heroes like this do exist  in the real world  without the capes and masks they're just  not as easy to recognize  instead they are ordinary people who  find themselves in extraordinary  situations at a crossroads with  significance far beyond themselves and  at that crucial point in the face of  peril resistance and personal risk they  decide to act for the good of all life  on earth  a hero holds the torch to see the way  forward towards a better future  can we see ourselves in this position  heroes from the past have much to teach  us let's meet some of them now  in october 1962 at the peak of the cuban  missile crisis vasili arquipov found  himself stationed on a soviet submarine  equipped with a nuclear torpedo that had  just been discovered by the us navy near  cuba  dying batteries had created overheated  conditions  sailors were fainting from lack of  oxygen in communication with moscow had  been cut off for over a week  when the americans dropped depth charges  to signal the soviet submarine to  surface both the captain and political  officer concluded that world war iii had  started  exhausted nervous and without radio  signal to clarify the situation the two  decided to launch a nuclear torpedo at  the us fleet despite the extremely tense  situation vasily arkhipov kept a cool  head  sensing that they were not under attack  he alone vetoed the launch thus his  torch led us away from a devastating  nuclear war between the ussr and the  united states  unfortunately the cold war tension  continued and by 1983 global nuclear  arsenals had more than doubled  enter our second hero stanislav pietrov  on a late september night he was on duty  observing the soviet early warning  network when the system alerted him of  five incoming american nuclear missiles  i just sat there for a few seconds  staring at the screen with the word  launch displayed in bold red letters but  i had a funny feeling in my gut he  suspected the missile detections were  false  with the world's destiny at his  fingertips he went with his gut rather  than blindly trusting the computer  and he was right  for the second time in a quarter century  the extraordinary resolve of a  responsible human led us away from the  unimaginable destruction of nuclear war  we've since learned that arkhipov and  pietrov saved us from even more harm  than they could have realized at the  time  now we know that an all-out nuclear  conflict can create a nuclear winter  extreme enough to cause global  starvation  the last century saw the emergence of  another technology with the potential to  wipe out billions of lives  bioweapons  in the 1960s matthew mazelson was  consulting for the us army when he was  shown vast quantities of anthrax being  manufactured for biological warfare  here meselson saw a major crossroads  humanity would either turn away from  this development or embark on an  unstoppable arms race in weapons of mass  destruction that were much cheaper than  nukes and much easier to make enabling  everyone from rogue states to terrorist  groups to get hold of them  going down this path would not only  tarnish biotech forever it could also  profoundly alter our humanity by  attacking our very dna  meselson decided to reject the fear and  panic taking humanity down that road  i felt a strong desire to get this thing  stopped  armed with all the facts he needed to  formulate a solid argument he managed to  convince henry kissinger president nixon  and most of the biology community to  embark on an alternative path  thanks to his conviction and  persuasiveness world leaders signed the  biological weapons convention in 1972  banning both their development and use  meanwhile another biological killer had  been ravaging the globe for centuries  smallpox was one of the worst diseases  ever to afflict our species  until two heroes confronted it  in 1958 soviet virologist viktor zhanov  with great persistence managed to  convince the world health assembly to  launch the smallpox eradication program  on which the usa and the soviet union  collaborated despite the cold war to  make this global effort a success  epidemiologist william fagey developed  innovative surveillance and containment  techniques enabling total eradication  while vaccinating only a small fraction  of the population  in 1980 smallpox was declared fully  eradicated which according to unicef has  saved over 200 million and counting  this success shows that making the world  a better place can involve many  persistent collaborators over many years  and collaboration was also vital in  saving the earth's ozone layer  ozone destruction and the resulting  damage to our crops skin and dna is  another horrific scenario we don't have  to live out thanks to the contributions  of three more heroes  joseph farman's remarkable antarctic  measurements led his team to discover an  alarmingly large and expanding hole in  the earth's protective ozone layer  susan solomon was the sherlock holmes of  atmospheric science cracking the long  unsolved puzzle of how cfcs chemicals  then widely used including infringes  spray cans and inhalers were destroying  ozone so fast over antarctica i was a  young scientist i was 29 at the time and  i decided that i was going to try to  take my photochemical model and make it  stand on its head and when all those  measurements were in  people got pretty convinced with the  world alerted and willing to act stephen  anderson of the environmental protection  agency stepped up and tirelessly guided  multiple industry sectors away from  using cfcs  all three contributed to the signing of  the montreal protocol in 1987 to date  the most successful international  environmental treaty ever  because of these heroes the ozone hole  is now closing  together these stories show how life on  earth can be defended and supported  through science global consensus  innovation and hard work  this takes individual heroes committed  to doing the right thing  to speaking up researching writing  persisting not for themselves not for  money power or glory but for all of us  and for everyone still to come  each person in these stories received  the future of life award recognizing  those who made the world a much better  place than it might otherwise have been  and now they pass the torches onwards a  new generation of heroes awaits let's  all light our torches and the path  towards a better future will be even  brighter  you

